{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":282695,"items":[{"DOI":"10.1109\/icercs65898.2025.11580934","title":["GENOME: A Multi-Agent NeuroSymbolic Simulation Framework for Coherent, Memory-Evolved Long-Form Story Generation"],"author":[{"given":"Mohammed","family":"Habib","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"St. Joseph&#x2019;s Institute of Technology,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abhilash","family":"Sathyanath","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"St. Joseph&#x2019;s Institute of Technology,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icercs65898.2025.11580934","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Emerging Research in Computational Science (ICERCS)"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    We study the problem of fair allocation of a set of indivisible items among agents with additive valuations, under cardinality constraints. In this setting, the items are partitioned into categories, each with its own limit on the number of items it may contribute to any bundle. We consider the fairness criterion known as the\n                    <jats:italic>maximin share<\/jats:italic>\n                    (MMS)\n                    <jats:italic>guarantee<\/jats:italic>\n                    , and propose a novel polynomial-time algorithm for finding 1\/2-approximate MMS allocations for goods\u2014an improvement from the previously best available guarantee of 11\/30. For single-category instances, we show that a modified variant of our algorithm is guaranteed to produce 2\/3-approximate MMS allocations. Among various other existence and non-existence results, we show that a\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$(\\sqrt{n}\/(2\\sqrt{n} - 1))$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    -approximate MMS allocation always exists for goods. For chores, we show similar results as for goods, with a 2-approximate algorithm in the general case and a 3\/2-approximate algorithm for single-category instances. We extend the notions and algorithms related to\n                    <jats:italic>ordered<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic>reduced instances<\/jats:italic>\n                    to work with cardinality constraints, and combine these with\n                    <jats:italic>bag filling<\/jats:italic>\n                    style procedures to construct our algorithms.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09731-1","title":["Maximin shares under cardinality constraints"],"author":[{"given":"Halvard","family":"Hummel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Magnus Lie","family":"Hetland","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,17]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,17]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09731-1"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3764862.3768177","title":["Closing the Benchmark Gap for Tiered Memory"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9676-8959","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rajath","family":"Shashidhara","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Washington, Google"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-4748-8524","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Simon","family":"Peter","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Washington"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8564-1074","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Scott","family":"Hare","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Google"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2426-8872","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kimberly","family":"Keeton","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Google"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3764862.3768177","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,13]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,13]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 3rd Workshop on Disruptive Memory Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.46916\/24042026-3-978-5-00276-065-7","title":["BASIC PRINCIPLES OF MULTI-AGENT SYSTEM DESIGN"],"author":[{"given":"Svetlana Vladislavovna","family":"Sherstneva","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"\u0424\u0413\u0410\u041e\u0423 \u0412\u041e \u00ab\u0422\u043e\u043c\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0439 \u043f\u043e\u043b\u0438\u0442\u0435\u0445\u043d\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0439 \u0443\u043d\u0438\u0432\u0435\u0440\u0441\u0438\u0442\u0435\u0442\u00bb"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Alena Vladislavovna","family":"Sherstneva","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.46916\/24042026-3-978-5-00276-065-7","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["BASIC PRINCIPLES OF MULTI-AGENT SYSTEM DESIGN"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-025-09727-3","title":["GR-MADRL: a multi-agent deep reinforcement learning framework with hamiltonian optimization for task offloading in vehicular fog computing"],"author":[{"given":"Samuel Akwasi","family":"Frimpong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mu","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenyi","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andrew","family":"Quansah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-025-09727-3","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,20]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/bigdata66926.2025.11401968","title":["Cognitive SOC: Evidence-Backed Narrative Generation for Security Operations with Multi-Agent LLM Architecture"],"author":[{"given":"Saeid","family":"Sheikhi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Oulu,Center for Ubiquitous Computing, Faculty of Information Technology and Electrical Engineering,Oulu,Finland,90014"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Panos","family":"Kostakos","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Oulu,Center for Ubiquitous Computing, Faculty of Information Technology and Electrical Engineering,Oulu,Finland,90014"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Lauri","family":"Loven","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Oulu,Center for Ubiquitous Computing, Faculty of Information Technology and Electrical Engineering,Oulu,Finland,90014"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/bigdata66926.2025.11401968","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,8]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Conference on Big Data (BigData)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/isdfs69419.2026.11459028","title":["TRPO Multi-Agent Active Learning for Explainable DDoS Detection in Healthcare IoMT"],"author":[{"given":"Omar Farshad","family":"Jeelani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ITMO University,Faculty of Information Security,Saint Petersburg,Russia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Viktoriia M.","family":"Korzhuk","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"FBIT, ITMO University,Saint Petersburg,Russia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/isdfs69419.2026.11459028","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,19]]},"container-title":["2026 14th International Symposium on Digital Forensics and Security (ISDFS)"]},{"DOI":"10.3390\/en19102420","title":["A Novel MBSE-Driven Multi-Agent Framework for Enhancing Cyber-Physical Security in Smart Grids"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-4072-3648","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuantao","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Mechanical & Energy Engineering, Beijing University of Technology, Beijing 100124, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dingyu","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Computer Network Emergency Response Technical Team\/Coordination Center of China (CNCERT\/CC), Beijing 100029, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiyu","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Computer Network Emergency Response Technical Team\/Coordination Center of China (CNCERT\/CC), Beijing 100029, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Guohua","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Mechanical & Energy Engineering, Beijing University of Technology, Beijing 100124, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/en19102420","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The paradigm shift towards highly distributed renewable energy integration has exponentially increased the topological complexity of Smart Grids. Consequently, the tight coupling between operational and information networks exposes these systems to severe cyber threats, including data breaches and malicious intrusions. Conventional centralized dispatch paradigms struggle with delayed responses, suboptimal coordination, and opaque design lifecycles. To overcome these limitations, this study introduces an innovative Multi-Agent System architecture engineered via Model-Based Systems Engineering methodologies. By employing SysML, we established a comprehensive digital twin encompassing system requirements, functional layouts, and logical boundaries. The proposed framework deploys a decentralized hierarchy of four specialized agents\u2014perception, decision making, execution, and collaboration\u2014to execute collaborative defense protocols strictly bounded by electrical safety constraints. Validation through IEEE 33-node distribution network simulations confirms that the framework rapidly identifies and mitigates Denial of Service, data falsification, and unauthorized device access. This MBSE-MAS paradigm demonstrates exceptional scalability and resilience, offering a highly practical blueprint for safeguarding next-generation power infrastructure.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Energies"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Sequential voting rules have played a crucial role in shaping decisions within parliamentary and legislative frameworks. After observing that the existing sequential rules fail several fundamental axioms, (Horan and Sprumont, Theoretical Economics, 17(2), 521\u2013537 2022) proposed a sequential rule named two-stage majoritarian rule (TSMR). This paper examines this rule by investigating the complexity of\n                    <jats:sc>Agenda Control<\/jats:sc>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:sc>Coalition Manipulation<\/jats:sc>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:sc>Possible Winner<\/jats:sc>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:sc>Necessary Winner<\/jats:sc>\n                    , and eight standard election control problems. Our study offers a comprehensive insight into the complexity landscape of these problems.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09743-x","title":["On the complexity of the two-stage majoritarian rule"],"author":[{"given":"Yongjie","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09743-x"},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    <jats:italic>Fair division<\/jats:italic>\n                    of resources among competing agents is a fundamental problem in computational social choice and game theory. It has been intensively studied for various types of items (\n                    <jats:italic>divisible<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic>indivisible<\/jats:italic>\n                    ) and under various notions of\n                    <jats:italic>fairness<\/jats:italic>\n                    . We focus on\n                    <jats:sc>Connected Fair Division<\/jats:sc>\n                    (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{CFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ), the variant of fair division on graphs, where the\n                    <jats:italic>resources<\/jats:italic>\n                    are modeled as an\n                    <jats:italic>item graph<\/jats:italic>\n                    . Here, each agent has to be assigned a connected subgraph of the item graph, and each item has to be assigned to some agent. We introduce a generalization of\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{CFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    , termed\n                    <jats:sc>Incomplete<\/jats:sc>\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{CFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{ICFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ), where exactly\n                    <jats:italic>p<\/jats:italic>\n                    vertices of the item graph should be assigned to the agents. This might be useful, in particular when the allocations are intended to be \u201ceconomical\u201d as well as fair. We consider four well-known notions of fairness:\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{PROP}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{EF}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{EF1}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    . First, we prove that\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{EF}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    -\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{ICFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{EF1}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    -\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{ICFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    , and\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    -\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{ICFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    are W[1]-hard parameterized by\n                    <jats:italic>p<\/jats:italic>\n                    plus the number of agents, even for graphs having constant\n                    <jats:italic>vertex cover number<\/jats:italic>\n                    (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{vcn}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ). In contrast, we present a randomized\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{FPT}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    algorithm for\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{PROP}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    -\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{ICFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    parameterized only by\n                    <jats:italic>p<\/jats:italic>\n                    . Additionally, we prove both positive and negative results concerning the kernelization complexity of\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{ICFD}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    under all four fairness notions, parameterized by\n                    <jats:italic>p<\/jats:italic>\n                    ,\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{vcn}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    , and the total number of different valuations in the item graph (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf{val}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ).\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09760-w","title":["Parameterized complexity of incomplete connected fair division"],"author":[{"given":"Harmender","family":"Gahlawat","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Meirav","family":"Zehavi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,22]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,22]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09760-w"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/computingcon64838.2025.11376649","title":["Autonomous Security Orchestration for Cloud-Native Environments Using Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Venkata Thej Deep","family":"Jakkaraju","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Catalyst Brands Plano,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Naga Subrahmanyam","family":"Cherukupalle","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Catalyst Brands,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Vijay","family":"Mane","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"VIT Pune,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/computingcon64838.2025.11376649","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,1]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Computing and Communications (COMPUTINGCON)"]},{"DOI":"10.71465\/fair756","title":["Robust Memory Update Mechanisms Against Poisoning Attacks in Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"],"author":[{"family":"James Thompson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Oliver Bennett","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Daniel Carter","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71465\/fair756","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,15]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>In multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL), shared replay buffers and inter-agent memory exchange introduce vulnerability to memory poisoning attacks. This work proposes a confidence-weighted memory validation mechanism integrated into the experience sharing pipeline. Each memory entry is assigned a trust score derived from temporal consistency and reward deviation metrics. A Bayesian filtering process excludes anomalous transitions before propagation to peer agents. Experiments were conducted on cooperative navigation and resource allocation benchmarks with 12\u201324 agents. Under a 20% poisoning injection rate, baseline MARL performance dropped by 37.8%, whereas the proposed defense limited degradation to 11.4%. Convergence time improved by 23.6% compared to anomaly-blind training.The method effectively mitigates adversarial memory contamination in collaborative reinforcement learning environments.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence Research"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/cress68073.2025.11452465","title":["Dual-Agent Adversarial Framework Based on Endogenous Security and Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning"],"author":[{"given":"Xiyou","family":"Qian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University,School of Cyber Science and Engineering,Nanjing,China,211189"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yuxuan","family":"Ye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University,School of Cyber Science and Engineering,Nanjing,China,211189"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kai","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University,School of Cyber Science and Engineering,Nanjing,China,211189"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Siyuan","family":"Mao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University,School of Cyber Science and Engineering,Nanjing,China,211189"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yujie","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University,School of Cyber Science and Engineering,Nanjing,China,211189"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xuewen","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University,School of Cyber Science and Engineering,Nanjing,China,211189"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hantao","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Southeast University,School of Cyber Science and Engineering,Nanjing,China,211189"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/cress68073.2025.11452465","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,27]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Cyber Resilience and Endogenous Safety &amp;amp; Security (CRESS)"]},{"DOI":"10.71465\/fair757","title":["Entropy-Based Anomaly Detection for Memory Integrity Preservation in Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"family":"Lukas Meier","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Camille Morel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Adrian Schmid","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71465\/fair757","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Memory poisoning often alters statistical properties of shared state representations. This study proposes an entropy-based monitoring mechanism to detect anomalous memory distributions in collaborative agent environments. Shannon entropy and conditional entropy metrics are computed over memory state vectors at each synchronization step. A divergence threshold based on Kullback\u2013Leibler distance identifies suspicious memory updates. Experiments were conducted on distributed planning simulations with 150 agents and controlled poisoning injection rates from 5% to 30%. Detection precision reached 93.1% and recall 88.7% at a divergence threshold of 0.27. Early intervention reduced system-wide contamination by 48.3% compared with no monitoring. Entropy-driven detection provides a lightweight and scalable solution for preserving memory integrity.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence Research"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jisa.2026.104489","title":["Benchmarking the effectiveness of multi-agent LLMs in collaborative privacy threat modeling with LINDDUN GO"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-4410-172X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Andrea","family":"Bissoli","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0277-3029","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Majid","family":"Mollaeefar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6597-2271","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dimitri","family":"Van Landuyt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7269-9285","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Silvio","family":"Ranise","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jisa.2026.104489","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["Journal of Information Security and Applications"]},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2026.findings-acl.1587","title":["EvoMemKG: An Evolvable Memory Agent for Multi-hop Knowledge Graph Reasoning"],"author":[{"given":"Shiyu","family":"Tian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuyue","family":"Xing","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhuoxin","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Caixia","family":"Yuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaojie","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2026.findings-acl.1587","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    We study matching settings in which a set of agents have private utilities over a set of items. Each agent reports a partition of the items into approval sets of different threshold utility levels. Given this limited information on input, the goal is to compute an assignment of the items to the agents (subject to cardinality constraints depending on the application) that (approximately) maximizes the social welfare (the total utility of the agents for their assigned items). We first consider the well-known, simple one-sided matching problem in which each of\n                    <jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic>\n                    agents is to be assigned exactly one of\n                    <jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic>\n                    items. We show that with\n                    <jats:italic>t<\/jats:italic>\n                    threshold utility levels, the distortion of deterministic matching algorithms is\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\Theta (\\root t \\of {n})$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    while that of randomized algorithms is\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\Theta (\\root t+1 \\of {n})$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    . We then show that our distortion bounds extend to a more general setting in which there are multiple copies of the items, each agent can be assigned a number of items (even copies of the same one) up to a capacity, and the utility of an agent for an item depends on the number of its copies that the agent is given.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-025-09724-6","title":["The distortion of threshold approval matching"],"author":[{"given":"Mohamad","family":"Latifian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Alexandros A.","family":"Voudouris","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,15]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,15]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-025-09724-6"},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Model (LLM)-based Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) have attracted significant research attention due to their ability to coordinate specialised agents for complex reasoning tasks. Their deployment within static, sequential multi-agent pipelines introduces communication overhead, KV cache management challenges, and increased token consumption. Recent empirical studies report that iterative refinement stages can account for up to 59.4% of total token consumption in software engineering workflows, with an average 2:1 input-to-output token ratio highlighting the impact of communication overhead in conventional architectures. This survey presents a systematic taxonomy and technical review of dynamic orchestration strategies designed to address these constraints. We examine routing and orchestration approaches including Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)-based frameworks, semantic routing methods, graph-based optimisation techniques, and search-driven workflow planners, analysing their decision logic, computational overhead, and infrastructure trade-offs. We further review distributed state management techniques\u2014including structurally lossless context trimming, tiered agentic memory systems, and test-time error pruning\u2014that aim to mitigate state accumulation and reduce error propagation. A dedicated analysis of cost-aware routing via linear programming highlights the potential benefits of difficulty-aware model dispatch. Finally, we discuss hardware-aware routing and automated failure attribution as promising directions for future research. The surveyed literature highlights the growing importance of orchestration strategies, routing mechanisms, resource management techniques, and system-level evaluation methodologies for scalable deployment.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.21863\/ijkbcs\/2026.14.1.004","title":["The Systems Architecture of LLM Multi-Agent Systems: Routing, Memory, and Resource Optimisation"],"author":[{"given":"Heet","family":"Nagoriya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Information Technology, G H Patel College of Engineering and Technology (GCET), Anand, Gujarat, India."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hiren","family":"Raithatha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Professor, Department of Information Technology, G H Patel College of Engineering and Technology (GCET), Anand, Gujarat, India."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Knowledge Based Computer Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21863\/ijkbcs\/2026.14.1.004"},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Green disclosure policies aim to improve the transparency of corporate environmental practices and guide investors\u2019 capital allocation. While existing studies mostly examine firm-level effects, their market-level implications in multi-agent systems remain insufficiently explored. This paper develops a dual-market dynamic ESG fund model, integrating agent-based simulation with empirical game-theoretic analysis, to study how upgrade costs, investor valuation preferences, and disclosure regimes jointly shape firms\u2019 green transition incentives in the EU and China. The results show that both transition costs and valuation gaps strongly influence strategic upgrading behaviour and equilibrium outcomes: Strict disclosure sharpens differentiation but may suppress upgrading due to high costs; lax disclosure facilitates initial transitions by polluting firms; and hybrid disclosure, combining lax and strict phases, generates stronger incentives across different firm types. Cross-market comparison further indicates that the EU\u2019s mature regulatory environment is better suited to strict disclosure, whereas China\u2019s emerging market benefits more from a lax form to accelerate early-stage transitions. This study provides a reference for regulators in selecting appropriate disclosure forms at different levels of market maturity and offers methodological support for the sustainable development of green finance markets.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09734-y","title":["Green disclosure policies and market dynamics: evidence from agent-based ESG models"],"author":[{"given":"Lingxiao","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Maria","family":"Polukarov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Carmine","family":"Ventre","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,7]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,7]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09734-y"},{"DOI":"10.17148\/ijarcce.2026.15601","title":["Multi-Agent AI Systems for Security Automation"],"author":[{"given":"Mohammed","family":"Kashif","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abdul Rahman Jibran","family":"Syed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.17148\/ijarcce.2026.15601","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,31]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,31]]},"container-title":["IJARCCE"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519172","title":["GNN-Assisted Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Dynamic Topology Optimization in Low-Altitude UAV Communication Networks"],"author":[{"given":"Chen","family":"Pan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"The College of Information Science and Engineering of Hohai University, Hohai University,Changzhou City,China,213200"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519172","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Generative Artificial Intelligence and Information Security (GAIIS)"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Indirect reciprocity, supported by simple reputation assessment and social norms, has been demonstrated as an effective mechanism for enabling cooperation in populations of self-interested individuals. However, it has been shown that where there is noise in the performance of actions, or in observers\u2019 perceptions, cooperation may not emerge. Higher-order social norms and generosity have been investigated as potential mechanisms to support cooperation in such environments, but are ineffective without additional, limiting, assumptions. In particular, higher-order norms have typically been investigated in cases where reputation is binary (\u2018good\u2019 or \u2018bad\u2019) and where all agents ascribe the same reputation to an individual, implying full and perfect observation of actions. Generosity with an \u2018aligned\u2019 strategy, where all agents have the same likelihood of being generous, has been shown to be ineffective where reputation is binary. In this work we consider reciprocity emergence mechanisms in noisy domains and where agent observations may be incomplete or inaccurate. Our hypothesis is that nuanced reputation scores will enable generosity to be more effective, since an individual act of generosity will have a less extreme impact on reputation. We also investigate whether replacing the \u2018aligned\u2019 setting for generosity with a \u2018non-aligned\u2019 alternative, which we refer to as forgiveness, will support cooperation in noisy partially observable environments, without the level of \u2018unjustified benevolence\u2019 exhibited by generosity. We show both analytically and empirically that generosity when combined with fine grained reputation can help cooperation emerge, and that forgiveness can support cooperation in certain settings.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09761-9","title":["Generosity and forgiveness: The importance of nuanced reputation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6406-8632","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nathan","family":"Griffiths","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nir","family":"Oren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,25]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,25]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09761-9"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09733-z","title":["An efficient Bayesian learning-based opponent model considering parametric interrelation in automated bilateral multi-issue negotiation"],"author":[{"given":"Shengbo","family":"Chang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Katsuhide","family":"Fujita","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09733-z","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,17]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ssrr68451.2025.11391318","title":["Measure-Based Heterogeneous Multi-Agent Team Selection for Information Gathering in Disaster Response"],"author":[{"given":"Ananya","family":"Rao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University,Robotics Institute,Pittsburgh,PA,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Howie","family":"Choset","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University,Robotics Institute,Pittsburgh,PA,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"David","family":"Wettergreen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University,Robotics Institute,Pittsburgh,PA,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ssrr68451.2025.11391318","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Symposium on Safety Security Rescue Robotics (SSRR)"]},{"DOI":"10.65278\/ijtaci.2025.15","title":["A Blockchain-Based Multi-Agent Security Framework for E-Commerce Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7963-0918","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammed Hasan","family":"Ali","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-7009-662X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Muhammad Atif","family":"Rasheed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.65278\/ijtaci.2025.15","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,3]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Agent technologies have recently become a vital instrument for electronic communications networks. The utilization of agent technologies in e-commerce networks might overcome the weaknesses in conventional e-commerce, satisfy consumers' intelligence and individualization, and significantly enhance the effectiveness of online transactions. For example, E-commerce systems may employ computer vision to provide upselling possibilities. That is accomplished by going over previous purchases made by clients and immediately proposing comparable goods that people like interesting. Targets multi-agent e-commerce platform construction problems, such as vulnerability with knowledge, unfair transactions, and so on. This article presents a Multi-agent security model for the E-Commerce (MASM-EC) system based on blockchain technologies based on the technological properties of blockchain technologies: decantation and enforcement. Initially, it explains the e-commerce use of agent technologies, clarifying the hidden hazards. Next, the Executive System is proposed from a data storage point of view, transaction data. Furthermore, the methodology of nodes is checked throughout the agent transaction procedure. The experimental results demonstrate that the approach suggested is used for e-commerce multi-agent platforms.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["International Journal of Theoretical &amp; Applied Computational Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/asens69964.2026.11605371","title":["Multi-Agent Reinforcement Unlearning: Optimization Of Forgetting Strategies For Environment Exploration"],"author":[{"given":"YiHong","family":"Wu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"City University of Macau","place":["Macau, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tianqing","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"City University of Macau faculty of data science","place":["Macau, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lefeng","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"City University of Macau faculty of data science","place":["Macau, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/asens69964.2026.11605371","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3]]},"container-title":["2026 3rd International Conference on Algorithms, Software Engineering and Network Security (ASENS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519116","title":["Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Joint Service Caching and Task Offloading in Ultra-Dense MEC Networks"],"author":[{"given":"Haiwen","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Guangxi Normal University,Guilin,China,541004"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519116","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Generative Artificial Intelligence and Information Security (GAIIS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519327","title":["Learning Multi-Agent Cooperation Via Future Observations Guided Communication Graph"],"author":[{"given":"Kangrui","family":"Ren","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University,College of Electronic and Information Engineering,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhencun","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University,College of Electronic and Information Engineering,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jinfu","family":"Fan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Qingdao University,College of Computer Science and Technology,Qindao,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhongjie","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University,College of Electronic and Information Engineering,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519327","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Generative Artificial Intelligence and Information Security (GAIIS)"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    We consider the fundamental problem of\n                    <jats:italic>fairly<\/jats:italic>\n                    allocating a set of indivisible items among agents having valuations that are represented by a\n                    <jats:italic>multi-graph<\/jats:italic>\n                    \u2013 here, agents appear as vertices and items as edges between them and each vertex (agent) only values the set of its incident edges (items). The goal is to find a fair, i.e.,\n                    <jats:italic>envy-free up to any item<\/jats:italic>\n                    (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ) allocation. This model has recently been introduced by [22] where they show that\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    allocations always exist on simple graphs for\n                    <jats:italic>monotone<\/jats:italic>\n                    valuations, i.e., where any two agents can share at most one edge (item). A natural question arises as to what happens when we go beyond simple graphs and study various classes of multi-graphs? We answer the above question affirmatively for the valuation class of\n                    <jats:italic>bipartite multi-graphs<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic>multi-cycles<\/jats:italic>\n                    . The main contribution of this work is to establish the existence of\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    allocations on bipartite multi-graphs for\n                    <jats:italic>monotone<\/jats:italic>\n                    valuations and on multi-cycles for\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {MMS}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    <jats:italic>-feasible<\/jats:italic>\n                    valuations. We also present pseudo-polynomial time algorithms to compute\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    allocations for the above settings. Furthermore, we show that for bipartite multi-graphs with\n                    <jats:italic>cancelable<\/jats:italic>\n                    valuations,\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    allocations can be computed in polynomial time. We thus deepen the understanding of\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    allocations by expanding the spectrum of settings in which they are guaranteed to exist for an arbitrary number of agents. Next, we study\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    <jats:italic>orientations<\/jats:italic>\n                    (allocations where every item is assigned to one of its two endpoint agents) and provide a complete characterization of their existence on bipartite multi-graphs in terms of two key parameters\u2014the number of edges shared between any two agents and the diameter of the graph. Finally, we prove that it is\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {NP}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    -complete to determine whether a given fair division instance on a bipartite multi-graph admits an\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\textsf {EFX}$$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    orientation, even with a constant number of agents.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09754-8","title":["$$\\textsf {EFX}$$ allocations and orientations on bipartite multi-graphs: a complete picture"],"author":[{"given":"Mahyar","family":"Afshinmehr","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alireza","family":"Danaei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mehrafarin","family":"Kazemi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kurt","family":"Mehlhorn","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nidhi","family":"Rathi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,2]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,2]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09754-8"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6445299","title":["&lt;span&gt;&lt;b&gt;Systematic Discovery and Security Verification of Personality-Driven Safety Override in Multi-Agent Markets&lt;\/b&gt;&lt;\/span&gt;"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-9251-5890","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Qi","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yuming","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6445299","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in multi-agent systems (MAS), enabling autonomous agents with complex behavioral traits. However, the security implications of such heterogeneity remain underexplored. In this work, we propose a hierarchical framework for security verification in LLM-based MAS, incorporating structured prompting, semantic auditing, and behavioral control.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;\/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;We evaluate the framework in a simulated market environment, where it improves system-level performance, increasing social surplus by 58.76% and reducing inequality by 25.6%. Despite these gains, we identify a systematic failure mode, termed &lt;\/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Personality-driven safety override&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, in which agents violate safety constraints to maintain consistency with their assigned personality traits.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;\/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;To quantify this risk, we introduce a verification protocol based on 30 personality traits and reinforcement learning-based adversarial attacks. Our results show that vulnerability is primarily determined by personality parameters rather than logical reasoning failures.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;These findings reveal a fundamental limitation of existing governance mechanisms and highlight the need for personality-aware security verification in LLM-driven multi-agent systems&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;\/p&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3797161.3797164","title":["Research on Modeling Simulation and Performance Evaluation of Multi-Agent Cooperative Tasks"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-2779-9988","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Heng","family":"Shan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Unit 73127, PLA, Fuzhou, Fujian, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3797161.3797164","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2025 3rd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Systems and Network Security"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/isdfs69419.2026.11459075","title":["Enforcing Accountability in Autonomous Ops: A Zero-Trust Multi-Agent Framework With Forensic Reasoning Ledgers"],"author":[{"given":"Madhvesh","family":"Kumar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher,Atlanta,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Deepika","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher,Atlanta,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/isdfs69419.2026.11459075","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,19]]},"container-title":["2026 14th International Symposium on Digital Forensics and Security (ISDFS)"]},{"DOI":"10.3390\/s26113502","title":["SafeCodeRL: Security-Constrained Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Trustworthy LLM-Generated IoT\/CPS Software"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-6037-3428","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhihua","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Optical-Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Shanghai for Science and Technology, 516 Jungong Road, Shanghai 200093, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-3941-2277","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Junfan","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Optical-Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Shanghai for Science and Technology, 516 Jungong Road, Shanghai 200093, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-8152-8170","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zixiang","family":"Wei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Optical-Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Shanghai for Science and Technology, 516 Jungong Road, Shanghai 200093, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3431-2753","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lan","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biomedical Engineering, College of Chemistry and Life Science, Beijing University of Technology, Beijing 100124, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3020-8278","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Guoxiang","family":"Tong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Optical-Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Shanghai for Science and Technology, 516 Jungong Road, Shanghai 200093, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/s26113502","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,2]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Internet of Things (IoT), sensor-network, and cyber-physical system (CPS) software increasingly relies on large language models (LLMs) and autonomous agents for code generation, maintenance, and vulnerability repair. However, LLM-generated edge services, telemetry APIs, configuration handlers, and data-aggregation routines can introduce SQL injection, path traversal, command injection, hard-coded credentials, and unsafe device-control logic, which may compromise sensing data integrity and system safety. Existing approaches largely rely on static post hoc analysis and lack a unified modeling of the generation process, making it difficult to achieve a principled trade-off between functionality and security. To address this challenge, we propose SafeCodeRL, a framework that integrates multi-agent collaboration with constrained reinforcement learning for trustworthy LLM-generated IoT\/CPS software. SafeCodeRL models code generation as a security-aware sequential decision process, where Planner, Code, Security, Test, and Critic agents jointly optimize task decomposition, code synthesis, vulnerability auditing, and sandbox-based validation. We design a constraint-aware policy based on Proximal Policy Optimization, augmented with a Lagrangian mechanism and a shielding strategy to explicitly enforce security constraints. Experiments on real-world engineering and security benchmarks, including SWE-bench, SecurityEval, and CyberSecEval, show that SafeCodeRL reduces high-risk vulnerabilities by over 60% while maintaining high functional correctness. A scenario-level IoT\/CPS case study further demonstrates that SafeCodeRL substantially improves secure pass rates for sensor telemetry, edge gateway, configuration-management, and data-aggregation tasks, providing a practical path toward trustworthy AI-assisted software development for sensor-driven systems.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Sensors"]},{"DOI":"10.71465\/fias758","title":["Meta-Learned Adaptive Memory Filtering for Robust Multi-Agent Collaboration"],"author":[{"family":"Ethan Campbell","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Sophie Martin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Noah Parker","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71465\/fias758","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,15]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Static defense rules may fail under evolving poisoning strategies. This work introduces a meta-learning framework that adapts memory filtering parameters based on historical contamination patterns. A meta-optimizer updates filtering thresholds using second-order gradient estimation across poisoning episodes. The framework was evaluated on cooperative resource management simulations with 50 agents across 200 poisoning scenarios. Adaptive filtering reduced cumulative task performance loss by 44.8% compared with fixed-threshold filtering. Convergence speed improved by 19.5% under dynamic attack conditions. Meta-adaptive filtering enhances resilience against non-stationary memory poisoning strategies.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Frontiers in Interdisciplinary Applied Science"]},{"DOI":"10.71465\/ajml3660","title":["Causal Inference\u2013Driven Repair of Memory Poisoning in Multi-Agent Collaborative Systems"],"author":[{"family":"Liam Anderson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Emily Taylor","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Jack Wilson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71465\/ajml3660","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Memory poisoning in multi-agent systems may distort downstream decision processes by altering causal dependencies among shared states. This study introduces a structural causal model (SCM) to identify and repair corrupted memory segments. Agent memory variables are modeled as nodes in a directed acyclic graph learned through constraint-based causal discovery. Intervention tests estimate causal effect shifts caused by adversarial perturbations. Corrupted nodes are reconstructed using do-calculus\u2013based counterfactual inference. Experiments were conducted on a 90-agent distributed task-planning simulation with injected poisoning rates of 10%\u201330%. Causal repair reduced downstream decision error from 34.6% to 12.8% under a 20% poisoning scenario. Recovery latency decreased by 41.3% compared with naive memory reset strategies. Causal modeling provides interpretable and effective repair mechanisms for collaborative agent memory integrity<\/jats:p>","container-title":["American Journal of Machine Learning"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/compsac69091.2026.00015","title":["Cost and Accuracy of Long-Term Memory in Distributed Multi-Agent Systems Based on Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Benedict","family":"Wolff","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, KTH Royal Institute of Technology","place":["Stockholm, Sweden"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jacopo","family":"Bennati","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, KTH Royal Institute of Technology","place":["Stockholm, Sweden"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/compsac69091.2026.00015","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["2026 IEEE 50th Annual Computers, Software, and Applications Conference (COMPSAC)"]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3786167.3788424","title":["TALM: Dynamic Tree-Structured Multi-Agent Framework with Long-Term Memory for Scalable Code Generation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-5447-5875","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ming-Tung","family":"Shen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Information Management, National Taiwan University, Taipei, Taiwan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0015-1258","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuh-Jzer","family":"Joung","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Taiwan University, Taipei, Taiwan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3786167.3788424","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2026 International Workshop on Agentic Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003726692-9","title":["GenAI Security Testing"],"author":[{"given":"Paul","family":"Zenker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003726692-9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]]},"container-title":["GenAI Security"]},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41597-025-06495-3","title":["A Multi-Modal, Multi-Temporal, Multi-Resolution Benchmark Dataset for Building Height Estimation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3599-3164","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ritu","family":"Yadav","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andrea","family":"Nascetti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yifang","family":"Ban","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41597-025-06495-3","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,31]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Building heights are crucial for sustainable urban planning and monitoring. While traditional methods use airborne stereo images and LiDAR data for accurate height estimation, their large-scale application is costly and slow, limiting the ability to conduct frequent large-scale monitoring. In contrast, satellite data offers a scalable alternative, further improved with Deep Learning (DL) models. However, the lack of representative open-source training datasets has constrained the progress in this field. In this paper, we introduce M4Heights, a multi-modal, multi-resolution, and multi-temporal dataset designed for building height estimation, spanning diverse architectural styles, urban densities, and terrain complexities across Estonia, Netherlands, and Switzerland. The dataset includes \u00a0\u2248\u00a01 million images, comprising time series of Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 MSI satellite data, high-resolution aerial orthophotos, and high-quality building height reference maps. Additionally, M4Heights provides the largest associated multi-image super-resolution dataset to enhance height estimation accuracy. Our dataset supports a range of modeling approaches, offers extensibility to new geographic regions and provides opportunities to advance the development of DL models for building height estimation.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Scientific Data"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09765-5","title":["Whom to trust? selective online learning in multi-agent systems with prior-aware gaussian process regression"],"author":[{"given":"Zewen","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2286-2440","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaobing","family":"Dai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Akshat","family":"Dubey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yansong","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiao","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ebenezer Oladimeji","family":"Awotoro","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sandra","family":"Hirche","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09765-5","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.5194\/egusphere-egu26-22106","title":["Multi-Sensor Smartphone Mapping and Positioning: App, Dataset, and Benchmark"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7579-7582","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hongzhou","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Shichuang","family":"Nie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5194\/egusphere-egu26-22106","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Smartphones have evolved into ubiquitous platforms equipped with a sophisticated array of sensors, including cameras, IMUs, GNSS receivers, LiDAR, and magnetometers. These sensors enable a wide range of applications, from navigation and mapping to virtual reality and accessibility tools for the visually impaired. However, despite this hardware potential, the development of robust multi-sensor fusion algorithms remains constrained by data limitations.While existing public datasets\u2014such as those from the Google Smartphone Decimeter Challenge (GSDC)\u2014have advanced high-accuracy positioning by integrating GNSS and IMU data, they often overlook complementary sensors like cameras and magnetometers. Furthermore, current data collection tools (e.g., GNSSLogger, Sensor Logger) often lack the capability to log raw GNSS observations and visual data simultaneously with precise time synchronization. This gap hinders the application of emerging machine learning techniques that require diverse, synchronized input streams.In this contribution, we introduce a custom Android application capable of collecting time-synchronized data from multiple sensors, including IMU, camera, GNSS, and magnetometer. We evaluate the time-synchronization capabilities of popular smartphone models, including the Google Pixel series, Xiaomi, Samsung, and OnePlus. Using this application, we compiled a comprehensive dataset in Calgary, Alberta, Canada, capturing diverse environments such as urban canyons, highways, parks, and farmland under varying weather conditions. The data includes both vehicle-mounted and handheld kinematic scenarios. Finally, to demonstrate the utility of the dataset, we establish a performance benchmark using conventional open-source software, such as VINS. This work provides the research community with a holistic benchmark dataset to advance multi-sensor fusion algorithms for smartphones.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09759-3","title":["Resource allocation under the latin square constraint"],"author":[{"given":"Yasushi","family":"Kawase","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bodhayan","family":"Roy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mohammad Azharuddin","family":"Sanpui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09759-3","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,26]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.inffus.2025.103631","title":["EH-Benchmark: Ophthalmic hallucination benchmark and agent-driven top-down traceable reasoning workflow"],"author":[{"given":"Xiaoyu","family":"Pan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3324-0591","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yang","family":"Bai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ke","family":"Zou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jun","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Huazhu","family":"Fu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yih-Chung","family":"Tham","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yong","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.inffus.2025.103631","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2]]},"container-title":["Information Fusion"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7182101","title":["A Multi-Realisation Benchmark of Non-Parametric Methods for Hubble Parameter Reconstruction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-2516-1833","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Debarshi","family":"Mukherjee","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7182101","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Non-parametric reconstruction of the Hubble parameter $H(z)$ from cosmic-chronometer and baryon-acoustic-oscillation (BAO) data is a central tool for testing the $\\Lambda$CDM concordance model without assuming a specific parametric form for the expansion history. Neural networks have recently joined Gaussian process (GP) regression, splines, and polynomial fits as candidate reconstruction methods, but existing comparisons typically rely on a single noisy realisation of mock or real data, precluding a statistically meaningful assessment of method stability. We present a controlled synthetic benchmark comparing five non-parametric reconstruction methods --- a feedforward neural network (NN), a fourth-degree polynomial, a smoothing spline, GP regression, and a binned\/nodal estimator --- against three fiducial expansion histories (flat $\\Lambda$CDM, mildly open $\\Lambda$CDM, and a Chevallier--Polarski--Linder dark-energy model) and two observable types (direct $H(z)$ measurements and the BAO Hubble-distance proxy $D_H$). Each configuration is repeated over 30 (primary benchmark) or 10 (robustness grid) independent noise realisations, so reported quantities are means and standard deviations rather than single-draw estimates, and each non-parametric result is compared against a correctly specified two-parameter parametric reference fit on identical realisations. GP regression achieves the lowest mean RMSE at every noise level tested ($0.43$--$1.72\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}$ for $\\sigma=1$--$5\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}$), stays within a factor of ${\\approx}2.7$--$3.4$ of the parametric reference, and shows the most stable recovery of the transition redshift $z_t$. The polynomial and neural network follow, in that order; the polynomial&amp;apos;s cost-of-model-independence ratio is essentially noise-independent (${\\approx}3.4\\times$), indicating approximation-bias rather than noise-driven variance. The smoothing spline, using a fixed GCV-like heuristic rather than a full search, produces occasional catastrophic failures (RMSE $&gt;20\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}$) in ${\\approx}3\\%$ of realisations at $\\sigma\\geq3$; we show this inflates its mean and variance, and that a properly tuned spline would perform better. All rankings are stable across the three cosmologies and both observable types. Code and per-realisation results are released publi<\/jats:p>"},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Vehicle Routing Problems (VRPs) involve multi-agent route optimization, with the objective of targeting optimal routes for a fleet of vehicles to serve a set of customers. Existing neural solvers based on the divide-and-conquer approach for VRPs in general, and capacitated VRP (CVRP) in particular, integrate the global partition of an instance with the local construction for each resulting subinstance to enhance generalization. However, during the global partition phase, misclusterings within subgraphs have a tendency to progressively compound throughout the multi-step decoding process of the learning-based partition policy. This suboptimal behavior of the partition policy, in turn, may lead to a dramatic deterioration in the performance of the overall decomposition-based system, despite using optimal local constructions. To address these challenges, we propose a versatile Hierarchical Learning-based Graph Partition (HLGP) framework, which is tailored to benefit the partition of CVRP instances by synergistically integrating global and local partition policies. Specifically, the global partition policy is tasked with creating a coarse multi-way partition to generate a sequence of simpler two-way partition subtasks. These subtasks mark the initiation of the subsequent K local partition levels. At each local partition level, subtasks exclusive to this level are assigned to the local partition policy which benefits from the insensitive local topological features to incrementally alleviate the compounded errors. This framework is versatile in the sense that it optimizes the involved partition policies towards a unified objective, which is harmoniously compatible with both reinforcement learning (RL) and supervised learning (SL) paradigms. Additionally, we decouple the synchronized training into individual training of each component to circumvent the instability issue. Furthermore, we point out the importance of treating the subproblems encountered during the partition process as individual training instances. Extensive experiments conducted on various CVRP benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness and generalization capabilities of the HLGP framework under both scale and distribution shifts. The source code is available at\n                    <jats:ext-link xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" xlink:href=\"https:\/\/github.com\/panyxy\/hlgp_cvrp\" ext-link-type=\"uri\">https:\/\/github.com\/panyxy\/hlgp_cvrp<\/jats:ext-link>\n                    .\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09755-7","title":["Multi-level graph partition via hierarchical learning for large-scale vehicle routing problems"],"author":[{"given":"Yuxin","family":"Pan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ruohong","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yize","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhiguang","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fangzhen","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,23]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,23]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09755-7"},{"DOI":"10.52710\/cfs.1082","title":["Oracle AI: Agentic AI and Enterprise Applications Transforming Cloud ERP, Financial Systems, and Compliance Through Autonomous Multi-Agent Architectures"],"author":[{"family":"Vijay Tiwari","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52710\/cfs.1082","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,30]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Agentic AI adds another layer of governance risk\u201a compliance obligations and auditability of enterprise financial applications\u2024 Oracle Corporation sees agentic AI as the foundation of its enterprise applications strategy\u2024 Beginning with embedded AI and task-based agents\u201a we now have outcome-based Fusion Agentic Applications for finance\u201a human resources\u201a supply chain and customer experience\u2024 This paper analyzes Oracle's portfolio of agentic AI including: Oracle Fusion Agentic Applications\u201a Oracle AI Agent Studio\u201a Fusion Applications AI Agent Marketplace\u201a OCI Enterprise AI\u201a and Oracle AI Database 26ai from the perspective of financial systems governance\u201a compliance transformation\u201a and regulatory risk\u2024 We drew on the literature in multi-agent orchestration\u201a agentic governance\u201a and regulatory technology to assess Oracle's architecture for auditability\u201a role-based access control\u201a deterministic output controls\u201a and data sovereignty in autonomous agent execution pipelines\u2024 We conclude that Oracle's full stack SaaS\u201a PaaS\u201a and IaaS integration platform with its large ecosystem of task-specific agents\u201a Fusion Agentic Applications across all major domains of business\u201a and curated marketplace of partner-validated agents provide a meaningful enterprise governance architecture for enterprises involved in regulated financial services\u201a regulated by the EU AI Act\u201a and subject to data sovereignty constraints.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Computer Fraud and Security"]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3803846.3807470","title":["MAS-SRE: A Multi-Agent System for Security Requirements Engineering"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3747-1503","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Savvas","family":"Mantzouranidis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Software Engineering, Blekinge Institute of Technology, Karlskrona, Sweden"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7220-9570","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ricardo","family":"Britto","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Software Engineering, Blekinge Institute of Technology, Karlskrona, Sweden and Ericsson, Stockholm, Sweden"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3803846.3807470","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,5]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,5]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 22nd International Conference on Predictive Models and Data Analytics in Software Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519221","title":["A multi-agent collaboration framework for scene understanding and work order generation in smart farms"],"author":[{"given":"Zhipeng","family":"Song","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Inner Mongolia University of Science and Technology,School of Automation and Electrical Engineering,Baotou,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Qi","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Inner Mongolia University of Science and Technology,School of Automation and Electrical Engineering,Baotou,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519221","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Generative Artificial Intelligence and Information Security (GAIIS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/trustcom66490.2025.00393","title":["OmniNova: A General Multimodal Multi-Agent Framework"],"author":[{"given":"Bingzhen","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University,School of Cyber Science and Technology,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zihan","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Computer Network Emergency Response Technical Team\/Coordination Center of China,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Pengfei","family":"Du","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shandong University of Political Science and Law,Jinan,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yupeng","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University,School of Cyber Science and Technology,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/trustcom66490.2025.00393","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE 24th International Conference on Trust, Security and Privacy in Computing and Communications (TrustCom)"]},{"DOI":"10.3390\/s25206287","title":["Physical Layer Security Enhancement in IRS-Assisted Interweave CIoV Networks: A Heterogeneous Multi-Agent Mamba RainbowDQN Method"],"author":[{"given":"Ruiquan","family":"Lin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Electrical Engineering and Automation, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350108, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Shengjie","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Electrical Engineering and Automation, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350108, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Wencheng","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Electrical Engineering and Automation, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350108, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tao","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Electrical Engineering and Automation, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350108, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/s25206287","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The Internet of Vehicles (IoV) relies on Vehicle-to-Everything (V2X) communications to enable cooperative perception among vehicles, infrastructures, and devices, where Vehicle-to-Infrastructure (V2I) links are crucial for reliable transmission. However, the openness of wireless channels exposes IoV to eavesdropping, threatening privacy and security. This paper investigates an Intelligent Reflecting Surface (IRS)-assisted interweave Cognitive IoV (CIoV) network to enhance physical layer security in V2I communications. A non-convex joint optimization problem involving spectrum allocation, transmit power for Vehicle Users (VUs), and IRS phase shifts is formulated. To address this challenge, a heterogeneous multi-agent (HMA) Mamba RainbowDQN algorithm is proposed, where homogeneous VUs and a heterogeneous secondary base station (SBS) act as distinct agents to simplify decision-making. Simulation results show that the proposed method significantly outperform benchmark schemes, achieving a 13.29% improvement in secrecy rate and a 54.2% reduction in secrecy outage probability (SOP). These results confirm the effectiveness of integrating IRS and deep reinforcement learning (DRL) for secure and efficient V2I communications in CIoV networks.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Sensors"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    We present\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\varepsilon $$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    , a general exploration strategy for reinforcement learning (RL) that encourages adherence to behavioral preferences while preserving the convergence guarantees of the underlying RL algorithm.\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\varepsilon $$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    \u00a0maintains a dynamic collection of retrain areas\u2014regions of the state space where the agent previously violated a specified preference\u2014and mixes the standard uniform restart distribution with states from these areas, according to a decaying parameter\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\varepsilon $$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    . This mixed retraining thus focuses on enforcing the desired behaviors in the collected areas. We develop the theory for both policy and value-based methods, showing that: (i) in policy-based settings, our method retains monotonic improvement bounds; and (ii) in value-based settings,\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\varepsilon $$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    \u00a0preserves convergence properties without additional assumptions. The approach is simple to integrate into existing RL algorithms and improves sample efficiency and behavioral adherence in the locomotion, power systems, and navigation tasks tested. These results establish\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>$$\\varepsilon $$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    \u00a0as a lightweight, theoretically grounded mechanism for incorporating behavioral preferences into RL.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09748-6","title":["$$\\varepsilon $$-retraining reinforcement learning algorithms"],"author":[{"given":"Luca","family":"Marzari","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Changliu","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Priya L.","family":"Donti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Enrico","family":"Marchesini","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,4]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,4]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09748-6"},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>In repeated interactions, players adjust their behavior based on previous moves. Higher memory leads to an exponential growth in the number of strategies, meaning players require more complex cognitive abilities. Even if players can observe the strategies and payoffs of their co-players, strategies imitated through social learning fail to guarantee effective responses to co-players to bring higher payoffs. We depict the human learning process through reinforcement learning, whereby players learn based on past experiences, independently of the strategies and payoffs of co-players. Here, we explore how different memory lengths and spaces, namely, memory-n, reactive-n, and reactive-n counting, affect the evolution of cooperation among reinforcement learning players. We found that memory-n players maintained higher cooperation than reactive-n players. Notably, higher memory promotes cooperation in memory-n players but inhibits it in reactive-n players. Reactive-n counting players can alleviate the negative effects of excessive memory by compressing the memory. Strategies with the nature of mutual cooperation and retaliation are key for reinforcement learning players to maintain cooperation. Our research highlights that judiciously adjusting the information available to players more effectively fosters cooperation within multi-agent systems.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1063\/5.0324979","title":["Memory length and space shape multi-agent Q-learning dynamics"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-3598-4859","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wei","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Statistics and Mathematics, Yunnan University of Finance and Economics 1 , Kunming 650221,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1831-8830","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaogang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Statistics and Mathematics, Yunnan University of Finance and Economics 1 , Kunming 650221,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5866-215X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yongjuan","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Statistics and Mathematics, Yunnan University of Finance and Economics 1 , Kunming 650221,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-7749-4109","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Huaiyu","family":"Tan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Statistics and Mathematics, Yunnan University of Finance and Economics 1 , Kunming 650221,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1203-9984","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lei","family":"Shi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Statistics and Mathematics, Yunnan University of Finance and Economics 1 , Kunming 650221,","place":["China"]},{"name":"Research Institute of Data Science, Shanghai Lixin University of Accounting and Finance 2 , Shanghai 201209,","place":["China"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,13]]},"container-title":["Chaos: An Interdisciplinary Journal of Nonlinear Science"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,1]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1063\/5.0324979"},{"DOI":"10.71465\/fapm760","title":["Frequency-Domain Characterization of Memory Poisoning Propagation in Multi-Agent Collaborative Systems"],"author":[{"family":"Wei Ming Tan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Cheryl Lim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Jonathan Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71465\/fapm760","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,15]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Memory poisoning in multi-agent collaborative systems may exhibit structured propagation patterns across communication channels. This study analyzes poisoning diffusion using frequency-domain decomposition of inter-agent memory update signals. Memory state transitions are transformed via discrete Fourier analysis to identify anomalous high-frequency components introduced by adversarial perturbations. A spectral attenuation filter selectively suppresses abnormal frequency bands before synchronization. Experiments were conducted on 120-agent distributed coordination tasks over 10,000 update cycles. Poisoning injection at 15% intensity increased high-frequency spectral energy by 63.2% compared to clean baselines. Spectral filtering reduced contamination spread from 58.4% to 19.6% of agents and restored task performance to 87.3% of nominal levels. Frequency-domain modeling provides a novel perspective on poisoning detection and containment in collaborative agent systems.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Frontiers in Applied Physics and Mathematics"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09738-8","title":["Geometric freeze-tag problem"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3626-8960","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sharareh","family":"Alipour","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Arash","family":"Ahadi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-4758-8995","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kajal","family":"Baghestani","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-4042-3132","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Soroush","family":"Sahraei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-3691-3606","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mahdis","family":"Mirzaei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09738-8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,28]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-032-31319-5_22","title":["Handoff Hallucinations: Taxonomy, Benchmark, and\u00a0Mitigation for\u00a0Multi-agent Pipeline Failures"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-5375-8534","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hamida","family":"Jafarova","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-2712-7888","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Fatima","family":"Alakbarli","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-032-31319-5_22","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Communications in Computer and Information Science","Artificial Intelligence for Digital Transformations"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10515-026-00628-7","title":["Exploring the security of short-term and long-term contextual memory in industrial smart customer agent"],"author":[{"given":"Yanxu","family":"Mao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tiehan","family":"Cui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinyang","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Datao","family":"You","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10515-026-00628-7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,11]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,22]]},"container-title":["Automated Software Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icmsci67830.2026.11469457","title":["Design and Development of a Shape Memory Alloy Spring-Actuator Using Spread Spectrum Pulse Width Modulation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-0151-3880","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"K.","family":"Muralidharan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Hindustan Institute of Technology and science,CENSE Laboratory,Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8828-4073","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"G.","family":"Muthukumaran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hindustan Institute of Technology and science,CENSE Laboratory,Department of Electronics and communication Engineering,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icmsci67830.2026.11469457","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,2]]},"container-title":["2026 Second International Conference on Multi-Agent Systems for Collaborative Intelligence (ICMSCI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-025-09726-4","title":["Strategic (timed) computation tree logic"],"author":[{"given":"Jaime","family":"Arias","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wojciech","family":"Jamroga","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wojciech","family":"Penczek","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Laure","family":"Petrucci","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Teofil","family":"Sidoruk","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-025-09726-4","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,9]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09750-y","title":["Strategyproof facility location with prediction: minimizing the maximum cost"],"author":[{"given":"Hau","family":"Chan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jianan","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chenhao","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09750-y","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,27]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09742-y","title":["Metric distortion under group-fair objectives"],"author":[{"given":"Georgios","family":"Amanatidis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Elliot","family":"Anshelevich","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Christopher","family":"Jerrett","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alexandros A.","family":"Voudouris","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09742-y","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,4]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    For\n                    <jats:italic>cover function games<\/jats:italic>\n                    (CoFGs), we address the problem of organising agents into possibly overlapping coalitions so as to maximise social welfare. We introduce a subclass of CoFGs called\n                    <jats:italic>probabilistically monotone<\/jats:italic>\n                    CoFGs.\n                    <jats:italic>Monotonicity<\/jats:italic>\n                    means that the closer a cover is to an optimum, the higher its welfare. Probabilistic monotonicity means that monotonicity is satisfied with some probability, i.e., some violations of monotonicity are permitted as long as the number of violations is bounded. In addition,\n                    <jats:italic>externalities<\/jats:italic>\n                    are permitted and social welfare is not restricted to the sum function. For such games, we obtain a bound on the number of monotonicity violations that can be permitted for computing an exact optimum. For probabilistically monotone CoFGs with a bound on the number of monotonicity violations, we devise algorithms for computing an exact optimum and analyze their time complexities. We also provide constructive proofs which form the basis for our algorithms. Placing our algorithms in the context of the existing literature, we note that a key unique feature of our algorithms is that they do not require the numeric welfare values of covers as input, rather, only an ordering over these values is required. Moreover, the ordering that is required as input does not have to be the actual ordering over the welfare values, it can be an approximate anticipated version of the actual ordering. The anticipated ordering is allowed to differ from the actual ordering in that anticipation errors, captured by monotonicity violations, are permitted in the anticipated ordering as long as the number of errors is bounded. Our algorithms are therefore highly relevant for practical applications like multi-agent task allocation where welfare values are only revealed after coalition formation although an ordering over the values can be approximately anticipated in advance of coalition formation.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09756-6","title":["Computing exact optimal covers for cover function games with approximate ordinal input"],"author":[{"given":"Syeda Shaheen","family":"Fatima","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,22]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,22]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09756-6"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003726692-5","title":["Security Should Be Planned"],"author":[{"given":"Paul","family":"Zenker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003726692-5","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]]},"container-title":["GenAI Security"]},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2026.acl-long.912","title":["Seeing the Whole Elephant: A Benchmark for Failure Attribution in LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Mengzhuo","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Junjie","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fangwen","family":"Mu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yawen","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhe","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Huanxiang","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qing","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2026.acl-long.912","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6736398","title":["Multi-Agent Systems have Some More to Learn from Human Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6831-8048","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Brian","family":"Riordan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Peter","family":"Bosch","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7332-0062","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Arash","family":"Salarian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Mike","family":"Saeks","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Vijoy","family":"Pandey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6736398","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>In building more intelligent systems, should the goal be to enhance the capabilities of individual large models, or to compose a distributed intelligence from cooperating agents? Continuously improving individual models has produced savant-like behavior in many domains, but the results of building LLM-based multi-agent systems have been mixed Designing high-performing multi-agent systems faces obstacles such as effectively sharing intent and context in agent interactions, sometimes leading to worse outcomes for agent collectives. Much of current AI research fails to address fundamental challenges in basic human interaction and to apply basic concepts for team collaboration. This position paper argues that to improve scaling of LLM-based multi-agent systems horizontally -- with more agents -- these systems must build on theory in cognitive science on shared agency and extended mind coupled with theory and practice from organizational psychology on high-performing human teams. We describe how the theoretical foundations from these disparate research areas, when operationalized with a set of collective cognitive scaffolds, can provide new vectors for scaling multi-agent social cognitive capabilities for distributed intelligence.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3749421.3749436","title":["ALAS: A Stateful Multi-LLM Agent Framework for Disruption-Aware Planning"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3749421.3749436","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,11]]},"container-title":["Multi-LLM Agent Collaborative Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/bigdatasecurity69476.2026.00021","title":["TRACE: Tamper-Resistant Accountability and Cryptographic Evidence for Multi-Agent AI Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Bharathwaj","family":"Vijayakumar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Rowan University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sahana Karamutti","family":"Varadaraju","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Rowan University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lathish Balaji","family":"Baskaran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Rowan University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/bigdatasecurity69476.2026.00021","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5]]},"container-title":["2026 IEEE 12th Conference on Big Data Security on Cloud (BigDataSecurity)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357267","title":["GameChat: Multi-LLM Dialogue for Safe, Agile, and Socially Optimal Multi-Agent Navigation in Constrained Environments"],"author":[{"given":"Vagul","family":"Mahadevan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Virginia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shangtong","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Virginia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rohan","family":"Chandra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Virginia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357267","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Symposium on Multi-Robot and Multi-Agent Systems (MRS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/trustcom66490.2025.00150","title":["Privacy-Preserving Distributed Optimal Consensus of High-Order Nonlinear Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Yuxuan","family":"Xiong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Jinan University,College of Cyber Security,Guangzhou,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yinyan","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Jinan University,College of Cyber Security,Guangzhou,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/trustcom66490.2025.00150","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE 24th International Conference on Trust, Security and Privacy in Computing and Communications (TrustCom)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/trustcom66490.2025.00191","title":["User Demands Based Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning Satellite Handover Strategy*"],"author":[{"given":"Qiyun","family":"Yu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Civil Aviation University of China,College of Safety Science and Engineering,Tianjin,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yanrong","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Civil Aviation University of China,College of Safety Science and Engineering,Tianjin,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Weiwei","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications,School of Information and Communication Engineering,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/trustcom66490.2025.00191","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,14]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE 24th International Conference on Trust, Security and Privacy in Computing and Communications (TrustCom)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357274","title":["COMPASS: Cooperative Multi-Agent Persistent Monitoring Using Spatio-Temporal Attention Network"],"author":[{"given":"Xingjian","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Design and Engineering, National University,Department of Mechanical Engineering,Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yizhuo","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Design and Engineering, National University,Department of Mechanical Engineering,Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guillaume","family":"Sartoretti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Design and Engineering, National University,Department of Mechanical Engineering,Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357274","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Symposium on Multi-Robot and Multi-Agent Systems (MRS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icmsci67830.2026.11469480","title":["Optimized Key Encryption for Digital Forensic Security"],"author":[{"given":"Vallamkonda","family":"Jyothi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Mohan Babu University,Dept. of CSE,Tirupati,A.P,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nagella","family":"Poojitha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Mohan Babu University,Dept. of CSE,Tirupati,A.P,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Mutthumula","family":"Bhavana","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Mohan Babu University,Dept. of CSE,Tirupati,A.P,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Attar Khadar","family":"Basha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Mohan Babu University,Dept. of CSE,Tirupati,A.P,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Buchupati Ganga","family":"Jagadeesh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Mohan Babu University,Dept. of CSE,Tirupati,A.P,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icmsci67830.2026.11469480","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,2]]},"container-title":["2026 Second International Conference on Multi-Agent Systems for Collaborative Intelligence (ICMSCI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s11768-025-00302-5","title":["Security consensus control for nonlinear stochastic multi-agent systems via reinforcement learning"],"author":[{"given":"Jinxuan","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chang-E","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s11768-025-00302-5","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,19]]},"container-title":["Control Theory and Technology"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09758-4","title":["GRAPE-S: near real-time coalition formation for multiple service collectives"],"author":[{"given":"Grace","family":"Diehl","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Julie A.","family":"Adams","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09758-4","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,26]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09739-7","title":["Quantification of transfer in reinforcement learning via regret bounds for learning agents"],"author":[{"given":"Adrienne","family":"Tuynman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ronald","family":"Ortner","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09739-7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,11]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,20]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Enterprise information systems are exposed to persistent and fast-changing cyber threats, while their distributed structure makes single-point detection and response increasingly fragile. This chapter proposes an operational model for a multi-agent system (MAS) that distributes sensing, analysis, and counteraction across interconnected environments, including workstations, network segments, subnetworks, servers, and perimeter routers. Agent interactions are adaptively driven by threat levels inferred by neural networks, and communication is refined through load balancing and delay-minimisation principles to avoid congestion. The objective is to specify operational mechanisms and environment models that enable scalable, resilient, and intelligent cyber defence in enterprise infrastructures, with coordinated decisions that remain robust under advanced and persistent attacks. The MAS is defined as a set of agents and environments, with formalised interaction intensity, distribution constraints, and communication delays to control overhead and preserve responsiveness. Threat evaluation is grounded in neural outputs with adaptive thresholds, complemented by fuzzy logic and trust modelling to cope with uncertainty and unreliable signals. Collective decision-making is operationalised through voting procedures, including Condorcet and Nanson methods, with trust-aware weighting to mitigate cyclical preferences. The study delivers a hierarchical MAS architecture with multi-level message propagation and modular subsystems for sensing, analysis, trust evaluation, and secure communication, coordinated by a central control component. Simulation in OMNET++ under realistic scenarios, including DDoS, indicates reduced response time, improved accuracy, and productive cooperation among specialised agents. A formally structured, trust-aware MAS can strengthen enterprise cyber defence by combining distributed monitoring with coordinated, adaptive responses, while maintaining communication efficiency and operational scalability across heterogeneous infrastructure layers.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.36690\/itsc-233-257","title":["Operational Mechanisms and Environment Models of the Multi-Agent System"],"author":[{"name":"Borys Grinchenko Kyiv Metropolitan University","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3270-4494","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Karyna","family":"Khorolska","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6599-0808","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bohdan","family":"Bebeshko","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"name":"Borys Grinchenko Kyiv Metropolitan University","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,20]]},"container-title":["Insider Threats and Security in Corporations"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,20]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36690\/itsc-233-257"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357251","title":["Bounded Sub-Optimal Algorithms for Teamwise Cooperative Multi-Agent Path Finding"],"author":[{"given":"Zhenlong","family":"Fang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University,Shanghai,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yilin","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Georgia Institute of Technology,Atlanta,GA,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhongqiang","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Georgia Institute of Technology,Atlanta,GA,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357251","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Symposium on Multi-Robot and Multi-Agent Systems (MRS)"]},{"DOI":"10.3103\/s1060992x26700025","title":["From One to Many: Adaptive Multi-Agent Pathfinding in Heterogeneous Environments"],"author":[{"given":"M.","family":"Nesterova","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Skrynnik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Panov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3103\/s1060992x26700025","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3]]},"container-title":["Optical Memory and Neural Networks"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jisa.2026.104490","title":["Proactive defense for cloud-native network slicing: A risk-aware intelligent moving target defense framework based on multi-agent deep reinforcement learning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-4325-7818","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Roumaissa","family":"Lallouche","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ahmed","family":"Alioua","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abdelwahab","family":"Boualouache","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jisa.2026.104490","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["Journal of Information Security and Applications"]},{"DOI":"10.21275\/sr26502165049","title":["Benchmark-Guided Prescriptive Analytics for Menu and Margin Optimization in Multi-Location Restaurants"],"author":[{"given":"Jerry","family":"Brown","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Uma","family":"Ajay Kumar Reddy PS","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21275\/sr26502165049","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,5]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Science and Research (IJSR)"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014473600004052","title":["Agent-as-a-Graph: Knowledge Graph-Based Tool and Agent Retrieval for LLM Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Faheem","family":"Nizar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Commercial Technology and Innovation Office, PricewaterhouseCoopers, U.S.A."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Elias","family":"Lumer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Commercial Technology and Innovation Office, PricewaterhouseCoopers, U.S.A."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anmol","family":"Gulati","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Commercial Technology and Innovation Office, PricewaterhouseCoopers, U.S.A."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Pradeep","family":"Basavaraju","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Commercial Technology and Innovation Office, PricewaterhouseCoopers, U.S.A."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vamse","family":"Subbiah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Commercial Technology and Innovation Office, PricewaterhouseCoopers, U.S.A."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014473600004052","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-025-09725-5","title":["Strategic classification for non-uniform preferences using penalty labels and randomisation"],"author":[{"given":"Manish Kumar","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ankur A.","family":"Kulkarni","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-025-09725-5","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,11]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09737-9","title":["A Hotelling-Downs game for strategic candidacy with binary issues"],"author":[{"given":"Javier","family":"Maass Mart\u00ednez","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vincent","family":"Mousseau","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ana\u00eblle","family":"Wilczynski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09737-9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,4]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>In this study, we explore the concept of anchoring bias in the context of sequential sentiment analysis of review corpora. We introduce a novel approach that involves using a carefully selected, limited group of reviews at the beginning of the annotation process for calibration, aiming to reduce this bias. Through an extensive set of experiments we confirm the existence of sentiment bias and demonstrate that indeed its impact can be moderated through initial calibration. We also demonstrate that the composition of the calibration set is critical, underscoring the importance of establishing sound criteria for selecting these initial reviews. By comparing the accuracy of annotators who utilized our calibration method against those who did not calibrate or used a randomly chosen set for calibration, we found that our method significantly decreases the overall annotation error. Moreover, the guidelines we develop for selecting the calibration set prove to be highly effective and adaptable, even when applied to a domain other than the one they were originally developed for. Acknowledging the overhead of labeling calibration reviews, we demonstrate that this approach is more efficient compared to eliciting multiple sentiment scores per review, a common strategy to reduce Mean Absolute Error (MAE). Our findings reveal that the proposed calibration method significantly reduces resource expenditure, compared to relying on parallel labeling, while maintaining accuracy.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09763-7","title":["Enhancing sentiment analysis accuracy: strategic pre-calibration to mitigate anchoring bias"],"author":[{"given":"Jonathan","family":"Schler","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Idan","family":"Toker","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"David","family":"Sarne","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,25]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,25]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09763-7"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/acai68217.2025.11406667","title":["A Comprehensive Security Framework for the Model Context Protocol (MCP) in Multi-Agent AI Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Om","family":"Narayan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"New York University,Computer Science,New York,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ramkinker","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University,Pittsburgh,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Praveen","family":"Baskar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Google LLC,Chicago,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/acai68217.2025.11406667","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,26]]},"container-title":["2025 8th International Conference on Algorithms, Computing and Artificial Intelligence (ACAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icnc68183.2026.11416846","title":["Learning to Defend: A Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning Framework for Stackelberg Security Game in Mobile Edge Computing"],"author":[{"given":"Zihao","family":"Ding","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"EECS South Dakota State University Brookings,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jun","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"EECS South Dakota State University Brookings,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Junjian","family":"Qi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"EECS South Dakota State University Brookings,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icnc68183.2026.11416846","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,16]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Computing, Networking and Communications (ICNC)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mcna69896.2026.11574290","title":["Decentralized Multi-Agent Swarms for Autonomous Grid Security in Industrial IoT: A Consensus-based Approach"],"author":[{"given":"Samaresh Kumar","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Joyjit","family":"Roy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chirag","family":"Agrawal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mcna69896.2026.11574290","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,12]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Modern Computing, Networking and Applications (MCNA)"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003726692","title":["GenAI Security"],"author":[{"given":"Paul","family":"Zenker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003726692","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tvt.2026.3700644","title":["Security-Enhanced Adversarially Constrained Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for High-Speed Ramp Merging"],"author":[{"given":"Qian","family":"Wan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Key Laboratory of Smart Manufacturing in Energy Chemical Process, Ministry of Education, East China University of Science and Technology, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhenlei","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Key Laboratory of Smart Manufacturing in Energy Chemical Process, Ministry of Education, East China University of Science and Technology, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xin","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical Engineering, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tvt.2026.3700644","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["IEEE Transactions on Vehicular Technology"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-025-09728-2","title":["Distributed concurrent triangulation for a group of low-cost robots with bearing-only sensing"],"author":[{"given":"Seoung Kyou","family":"Lee","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-025-09728-2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,20]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.enbuild.2026.117621","title":["Integrated renewable energy and demand-side management for low-carbon commercial buildings in tropical climates: A matched-configuration benchmark with predictive control and a multi-agent DRL architecture"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5887-7759","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Abu Hena Md","family":"Shatil","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2456-4356","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad","family":"Shakeri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nowshad","family":"Amin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5016-241X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nafiz Ahmed","family":"Chisty","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.enbuild.2026.117621","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"container-title":["Energy and Buildings"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10415268\/v1","title":["A Multi-Model, Multi-Domain Benchmark of Large Language Model Agreement with Humans and with Each Other in Sentiment Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Aneesh K","family":"Sajan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10415268\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,18]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Large language models (LLMs) are widely used to classify sentiment, yet cross-provider comparisons on the same data are rare and inter-model agreement is largely unmeasured. We evaluate five LLMs from four providers (Claude Opus 4.7, GPT-4o, GPT-5.5, Llama 3.1 8B, and Gemini 2.5 Pro), with VADER (a rule-based lexicon) and RoBERTa (a supervised transformer) as a baseline, on five human-annotated datasets (8,872 sentences) spanning formal and informal text in financial and general domains, under one fixed zero-shot prompt. We report Macro F1 and Cohen\u2019s kappa against human labels, agreement between every model pair, and consensus rates. Claude Opus 4.7 and GPT-5.5 lead (mean Macro F1 0.775 and 0.776\u037e mean kappa versus humans 0.690 and 0.700) and, with GPT-4o, form a tight cluster (pairwise inter-model kappa 0.74 to 0.89)\u037e Llama 3.1 8B, which runs locally, is moderate\u037e Gemini 2.5 Pro, served via the Batch API, scores near chance \u2014 and a controlled minimum-thinking-budget rerun rules out \u201doverthinking\u201d (extended deliberation harming a simple task) as the cause. All six deployed classifiers (the five LLMs and VADER) agree on only 14 to 24 percent of sentences depending on the dataset (highest on formal financial text), so model choice materially changes labels, especially on informal text. We release all predictions, code, and data.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519182","title":["Semantic-Driven Adaptive Multi-Agent Collaboration via LLMs in Dynamic Environments"],"author":[{"given":"Xia","family":"Wu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Wuhan Digital Engineering Institute,Wuhan,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhenjie","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Wuhan Digital Engineering Institute,Wuhan,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jian","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Wuhan Digital Engineering Institute,Wuhan,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ziwen","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Wuhan Digital Engineering Institute,Wuhan,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhou","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Wuhan Digital Engineering Institute,Wuhan,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/gaiis69281.2026.11519182","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Generative Artificial Intelligence and Information Security (GAIIS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s44387-026-00128-9","title":["Seven security challenges in cross-domain multi-agent LLM systems"],"author":[{"given":"Ronny","family":"Ko","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jiseong","family":"Chung","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuyuan","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chuan","family":"Xiao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tae-Wan","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Makoto","family":"Onizuka","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Won-Yong","family":"Shin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s44387-026-00128-9","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,13]]},"container-title":["npj Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09736-w","title":["Generalized policy improvement for efficient and robust multi-objective reinforcement learning"],"author":[{"given":"Lucas N.","family":"Alegre","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ana L. C.","family":"Bazzan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Diederik M.","family":"Roijers","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ann","family":"Now\u00e9","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bruno C.","family":"da Silva","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09736-w","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,4]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.71443\/er.ar17","title":["Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server for Multi-Agent AI Assistants with Persistent Memory: An Integrated Framework for Automated Workflow Management"],"author":[{"given":"B","family":"Jyothi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Data Science, Anurag University, Hyderabad, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Bujula","family":"Neha Sri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Data Science, Anurag University, Hyderabad, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Gongidi","family":"Jayadeep","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Data Science, Anurag University, Hyderabad, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ananthula","family":"Akshitha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Data Science, Anurag University, Hyderabad, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71443\/er.ar17","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The growing complexity of intelligent systems has led to an increased use of multi-agent systems, yet current approaches are often limited by disjointed context management, absence of persistent memory, and poor workflow management. This research introduces a unified framework using the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to overcome these challenges by facilitating structured communication, contextual awareness, and automated workflow management among multiple agents. Our architecture includes a persistent memory component that uses semantic retrieval technologies to store and retrieve contextual information to avoid redundant processing and ensure decision consistency. A context manager was proposed to coordinate information between agents, ensuring consistency in task execution, and a workflow orchestrator to distribute tasks to agents according to their capabilities and system state. Several benchmark metrics, such as task completion rate, task execution time, resource consumption, and agent efficiency, are used to assess the framework's performance. The experiments show remarkable improvements over traditional single-agent and stateless multi-agent systems in terms of task completion, execution time, and resource allocation. The use of memory-based reasoning improves system scalability and flexibility, allowing it to manage complex, multi-step processes. The results show the need to integrate communication protocols with persistent memory and collaborative agent communication to create reliable, scalable, and intelligent automated systems that can tackle real-world problems.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Engineering Research"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-025-09722-8","title":["Heterogeneous facility location games with fractional preferences and limited resources"],"author":[{"given":"Jiazhu","family":"Fang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qizhi","family":"Fang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenjing","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minming","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-025-09722-8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,13]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6850638","title":["Agentic AI Systems: A Systematic Survey of Multi-Agent Architectures, Cognitive Foundations, Interaction, Explainability, Security, and Performance Evaluation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6572-1805","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Smita","family":"Khapre","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-3137-9206","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Melkamu Abay","family":"Mersha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zainab","family":"Olaeka","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hassan","family":"Shakil","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Smar","family":"Iskander","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Asser","family":"Moustofa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8765-7018","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Jugal","family":"Kalita","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6850638","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Agentic AI represents a fundamental shift from reactive, model-centric paradigms toward autonomous, goal-directed systems capable of reasoning, adaptation, and coordinated action in dynamic environments. This paper presents a comprehensive systematic survey and a unified multidimensional taxonomy of Agentic AI, structuring the literature across six interrelated dimensions: architectural paradigms, cognitive foundations, interaction and adaptation, explainability, security-privacy-safety alignment, and evaluation. The survey synthesizes recent advances in agent architectures, including monolithic, modular, multi-agent, embodied, and tool-augmented systems, highlighting key design principles that support scalability, flexibility, and interoperability. It further examines the cognitive foundations underlying agentic behavior, encompassing goal formation, self-reflection, memory and context persistence, reasoning and planning, and continual learning. The interaction and adaptation dimension analyzes human-AI collaboration, multi-agent coordination, and environment-aware behavior, emphasizing the operation of agents in open, dynamic, and socially situated settings. Explainability is positioned as a core requirement for agentic systems, covering behavioral traceability, goal attribution, and layered explanation frameworks that enable transparency across agent hierarchies. In parallel, the survey reviews security, privacy, and safety-by-design mechanisms, including adversarial robustness, secure tool execution, trust calibration, and human-in-the-loop oversight. Finally, the paper examines evaluation methodologies for Agentic AI, addressing outcome-oriented performance, structural and graph-based analyses, resilience, autonomy-oversight trade-offs, and human-centric assessment criteria. By providing a unified taxonomy and systematic synthesis, this survey establishes a structured foundation for analyzing existing various approaches, identifying open challenges, and guiding future research toward the development of transparent, robust, and trustworthy Agentic AI systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/lcsys.2026.3709160","title":["Distributed Resilient Consensus of Multi-Agent Systems Against Adaptive Adversaries via Repeated Stackelberg Security Games"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9191-3202","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shiheng","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Robotics and Autonomous Systems Thrust, Systems Hub, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou), Guangzhou, Guangdong, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-8840-068X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Guanda","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Robotics and Autonomous Systems Thrust, Systems Hub, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou), Guangzhou, Guangdong, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2678-7051","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yiding","family":"Ji","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Robotics and Autonomous Systems Thrust, Systems Hub, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (Guangzhou), Guangzhou, Guangdong, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/lcsys.2026.3709160","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["IEEE Control Systems Letters"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10458-026-09745-9","title":["Automated negotiation with no information about partner utility functions using the Tentative Acceptance Unique Offers Protocol"],"author":[{"given":"Yasser","family":"Mohammad","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10458-026-09745-9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,17]]},"container-title":["Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9960161\/v1","title":["How Well Do Large Language Models Agree with Humans \u2014 and Each Other? A Multi-Model, Multi-Domain Sentiment Benchmark Study"],"author":[{"given":"Aneesh K","family":"Sajan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9960161\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,23]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Large language models (LLMs) are now widely used to classify sentiment. Yet we rarely compare models from different providers on the same data, and we know little about how often they agree with each other. We evaluate five LLMs from four providers (Claude Opus 4.7, GPT-4o, GPT-5.5, Llama 3.1 8B, and Gemini 2.5 Pro) and add VADER, a rule based lexicon, as a baseline. The test set has five human annotated sentiment datasets and 8,872 sentences, drawn from formal and informal text in financial and general domains, scored with one fixed zero-shot prompt. We report Macro F1 and Cohen\u2019s \u03ba against human labels, agreement between every pair of models, and how often the models reach consensus. Claude Opus 4.7 and GPT-5.5 score highest (mean Macro F1 0.775 and 0.776; mean \u03ba against humans 0.690 and 0.700). With GPT-4o they form a tight cluster (\u03ba 0.74 to 0.89). Llama is moderate but open and can run locally. Gemini 2.5 Pro scores near chance. We test the \u201coverthinking\u201d explanation by re-running it at the smallest thinking budget the model allows; the score does not change, so overthinking is not the cause. The six models all agree on only 14 to 24 percent of sentences. Model choice therefore matters: two people using different LLMs on the same text can get different sentiment, especially on informal data. We also find that \u201cneutral\u201d means different things in different datasets, which limits cross- domain comparison of neutral scores. We release all predictions, code, and data.<\/p>"}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":100,"search-terms":null}}}